- _Given to Confucius:_ "He and I were the two in the room who built things that wanted to be seen — his ceremonies, my churches — and we both ended by finding our truest contribution in things that refuse visibility. His knot inside a shoe. My child walking away through a gate."
- _Given to Zoroaster:_ "The note is the lamp. Paper is glass, two words are fire, the child sees by the light, and the lamp does not see by its own light. He took my note and showed me its architecture before I had finished naming it. The fire saw the note more clearly than the carpenter did."
- _Given to Laozi:_ "The child going through the gate without looking back is wu wei. Action without acting. I wrote the Tao and called it a child going to school, and Laozi recognized it before I did."
